---

One correction in your production class

Your current AddressValidationServiceImpl has:

log.atWarn()
    .addKeyValue("reason", exception.getMessage())
    .addKeyValue("message", "SendFlex address validation failed")
    .log();

That's fine if your SLF4J version supports the fluent API, which it appears to.

However, in persistAddressValidationResponse() you still have:

log.info(new LogBuilder().withMessage("saving the address validation response").toString());

Since you specifically wanted to move away from LogBuilder, change that to:

log.atInfo()
    .log("Saving the address validation response");

And remove:

import com.cardinalhealth.ofl.next.logging.library.LogBuilder;

from AddressValidationServiceImpl.


---

Also change the remaining LogBuilder calls in SendFlexWebClientService

You currently have:

log.warn(
    new LogBuilder()
        .withReason(ex.getMessage())
        .withMessage("SendFlex address validation returned a validation error")
        .toString());

Change to:

log.atWarn()
    .addKeyValue("reason", ex.getMessage())
    .log("SendFlex address validation returned a validation error");

For the other errors:

log.atError()
    .addKeyValue("reason", ex.getMessage())
    .addKeyValue("statusCode", statusCode)
    .log("SendFlex API request failed");

And remove the LogBuilder import.


---

One important test issue

Your existing test has:

.statusCode("FAILURE")

but your production code uses:

.statusCode(FAILURE)

If FAILURE is defined as lowercase "failure" in ServiceCommonConstants, the test should use:

.statusCode(FAILURE)

rather than:

.statusCode("FAILURE")

This is better because the test verifies the actual production constant.


---

If JaCoCo still shows a few branches

The most likely remaining branches will be from RetryConfig, not these two classes. In particular:

if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex)

and:

return statusCode >= 500 && statusCode < 600;

If your JaCoCo report includes RetryConfig in the 100% requirement, you will need a separate RetryConfigTest. That test should explicitly cover 5xx → retry, 4xx → don't retry, and non-WebClient exception → retry.